\f0\fs32 \cf2 One advantage of collecting data via an API is that APIs provide structured and stable access to data. They return data in standardized formats such as JSON or CSV, which reduces errors caused by changes in webpage layouts that often break web scrapers. A second advantage is that APIs often provide access to backend data and metadata that may not be fully visible on public webpages, allowing researchers to retrieve larger and more systematic datasets.\
\
However, APIs also present risks. First, API access may introduce coverage bias because researchers can only retrieve variables and records that the provider chooses to expose. Second, APIs can change over time through endpoint updates, version changes, or rate limits, which may break data pipelines and reduce reproducibility.\
\
To document API-based data provenance, researchers should record the API provider, endpoint URLs, query parameters, API version, authentication method, and the exact date of data collection. They should also archive the raw data files and the code used to query the API so that others can reproduce the dataset even if the API changes later.}